\label{app:ci_coverage}

We assess empirical coverage of 95\% posterior credible intervals via Monte Carlo simulation. At $N{=}8$ (200 replications), ability parameter $\theta$ achieves 90.0\% coverage while contamination susceptibility $c_n$ reaches only 65.6\%. Increasing to $N{=}20$ (51 replications) yields comparable rates: 89.1\% for $\theta$ and 64.7\% for $c_n$. The near-identical $c_n$ undershoot across sample sizes indicates that the coverage gap is structural rather than a finite-sample artifact: the Murphy--Topel asymptotic correction converges slowly for parameters that enter the likelihood through the multiplicative coupling $c_n \cdot e_i$, where the HalfNormal prior's boundary constraint at $c_n = 0$ further compounds posterior concentration. These results support treating $c_n$ point estimates and rank orderings as reliable diagnostics, while noting that $c_n$ credible intervals are systematically too narrow by approximately 30 percentage points.

